// `create_clock` defines the period the tool checks every path against;
// nothing is analysed until a clock {{c1::exists}}.
// `set_clock_groups -asynchronous` tells the tool that two clocks have
// no fixed {{c2::phase}} relationship, so crossings between them are not
// analysed as ordinary paths - this is what a CDC design needs.
// `set_input_delay` and `set_output_delay` describe what the {{c3::board}}
// guarantees at the pins. A path with no constraint is not analysed at
// all, so it is {{c4::invisible}} rather than passing.

// Setup slack is {{c1::required}} arrival time minus actual arrival time; a
// positive number means the path meets timing with that much margin.
// The required time is the clock period, {{c2::plus}} the clock skew at the
// capture flop, minus the uncertainty, minus the library setup time.
// The arrival time is clock-to-Q plus the {{c3::logic and net}} delay.
// Skew that helps the setup check by the same amount {{c4::hurts}} the hold
// check on that path - it appears with the opposite sign.

// A false path is one no functional operation ever uses, so it is
// {{c1::excluded}} from analysis; a multicycle path is genuinely used but is
// allowed {{c2::N}} clock periods instead of one.
// Declaring `set_multicycle_path N -setup` without the matching
// `-{{c3::hold}} N-1` is the classic error: relaxing setup by N cycles moves
// the hold check N-1 cycles later as well, so the tool then demands
// impossible {{c4::hold}} margin and inserts enormous buffering.
// Both statements are promises. A wrong one hides a real violation.

